A dictionary in Python is a powerful and flexible data structure used to store data in key-value pairs.
________________________________________ 
What Is a Dictionary?
•	A dictionary is unordered (until Python 3.7, now it's ordered), mutable, and indexed by keys.
•	Keys must be unique and immutable (e.g., strings, numbers, tuples).
•	Values can be of any data type and can be duplicated.
________________________________________ 
Creating a Dictionary
# Basic dictionary
person = {
    "name": "Alice",
    "age": 30,
    "city": "New York"
}

# Using dict() constructor
person2 = dict(name="Bob", age=25, city="Chicago")
________________________________________
Accessing and Modifying Values
# Access value by key
print(person["name"])  # Output: Alice

# Modify value
person["age"] = 31

# Add new key-value pair
person["email"] = "alice@example.com"
________________________________________ 
Removing Items
# Remove a key
del person["city"]

# Use pop() to remove and return value
email = person.pop("email")
________________________________________
Looping Through a Dictionary
for key, value in person.items():
    print(f"{key}: {value}")
________________________________________
Method In Dictonary

Useful Methods
Method	     Description
keys()	     Returns all keys
values()	   Returns all values
items()	     Returns key-value pairs
get(key)	   Returns value for key, or None if missing
update()	   Adds or updates multiple key-value pairs
clear()      Clear all dictonary output empty dict
pop()        skip specific values in dict.
del()        delete dictonary
